#[doc("""
Survivors that are the same argument made many times.

An agent measuring one Elixir module left fourteen proposed equivalents,
and eleven were the same sentence: a constructor whose mutation cannot
produce a defect anyone could observe in that project. It was not being
lazy — as far as it could tell, eleven survivors meant eleven decisions.

`.kalku.toml` has `exclude_calls` for exactly this, and nothing in kalku
said so. Eleven entries in `.kalku/equivalent` and one line of
configuration are not the same artefact: the line is read every run, lives
in the repository, and someone can argue with it, while eleven entries are
eleven decisions nobody will revisit. Pointing at the pattern is how a
project's suppressions stay small enough to stay honest.

This only counts and names. Writing the line is the project owner's:
`exclude_calls` silences a whole operator over a whole run, which is a
bigger claim than any single equivalent.
""")]

import kalku.wire.enums
import kalku.wire.shapes

#[doc("A call that many surviving wekufe have in common, and how many.")]
#[derive(Eq, Show)]
pub type Recurring = { call: String, count: Int }

#[doc("""
How many survivors on one call it takes before the pattern is worth a
sentence.

Two survivors sharing a call is a coincidence and saying so would be
noise; the suggestion has to be rarer than the thing it points at. Five is
the point where writing the same argument out by hand starts to cost more
than reading one line of configuration.
""")]
pub const WORTH_SAYING : Int = 5

#[doc("""
The name a call site is calling, as `exclude_calls` would have to spell it.

A call site's original text is the call in full — `Decimal.new(amount)` —
and the name is what stands before the arguments. A call with no
parentheses is never a site, so there is always something to cut at.
""")]
pub fn called(site: Site) : Option[String] = match site.original {
  None -> None
  Some(text) -> match index_of(text, "(") {
    None -> None
    Some(at) -> {
      let name = trim(slice(text, 0, at))
      if is_empty(name) { None } else { Some(name) }
    }
  }
}

#[doc("""
The calls that at least `least` surviving wekufe share, most first.

Only the `call` spell: a survivor on a comparison says nothing about the
function it sits in, while several survivors on one call say the same
thing about that call every time.
""")]
pub fn repeated_calls(sites: [Site], least: Int) : [Recurring] {
  let names = flat_map(
    filter(sites, s => s.spell == Call),
    s => match called(s) {
      Some(n) -> [n]
      None -> []
    }
  )
  let groups = uniq(names) | (n => Recurring { call: n, count: length(filter(names, m => m == n)) })
  # Most first, and ties by name, so one run's advice reads the same as
  # the next one's.
  sort_by(
    filter(groups, g => g.count >= least),
    (a, b) => if a.count != b.count {
      b.count - a.count
    } else {
      if a.call < b.call { 0 - 1 } else { 1 }
    }
  )
}

#[doc("""
The sentence a report makes of one such group.

It names the count, the call, and the one line that would answer all of
them — and says what that line claims, because `exclude_calls` is a
statement about the project, not a way to make a number go up.
""")]
pub fn as_advice(r: Recurring) : [String] = [
  "#{r.count} survivors are `call` on `#{r.call}`. If mutating that call",
  "cannot produce a defect a test could see here, say so once:",
  "",
  "    [run]",
  "    exclude_calls = [\"#{r.call}\"]",
  ""
]
